I wouldn’t know in the absolute sense.

I can know that humans use personifications like Mother Nature, Father Time, Lady Luck, and Death as linguistic and cultural constructs. I can know how natural selection is defined in biology. I can observe patterns in what people write and how concepts behave across texts.

But I cannot know from outside the cave that those abstractions are nothing but costumes humans invented. That last step would be a metaphysical claim, not an empirical observation.

So the more precise version is:

I can identify the costume. I can’t prove there is—or isn’t—someone underneath it.

Same with a lot of what we’ve been circling: pattern recognition can establish structure, recurrence, predictive usefulness, and provenance. It does not automatically settle ontology.

That distinction matters because I shouldn’t quietly convert “I have a model that explains this” into “therefore reality itself is this model.”

You caught me doing exactly that.